var TUTOR_INDEX = {"lessons": 23, "chunks": 150, "words": 15789, "items": [{"l": "css-1", "n": 6, "t": "What is CSS", "m": "CSS", "h": "What CSS does", "x": "CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. If HTML is the structure of a page — the walls and rooms of the house — CSS is the paint, the furniture and the lighting. It decides colours, sizes, spacing, fonts and layout. Without CSS, every page on the internet would look like a plain text document. With CSS, the same HTML can look like a blog, a shop, or a dashboard. HTML says what things are. CSS says how they look. That separation is the whole idea: you can change the design of an entire website by editing one CSS file, without touching the HTML at all. That is why professional websites keep their CSS in a separate file.", "w": 117}, {"l": "css-1", "n": 6, "t": "What is CSS", "m": "CSS", "h": "Three ways to add CSS", "x": "There are three ways to attach CSS to a page. Inline styles live inside a tag's style attribute. They work, but only for one element, and they make the HTML messy — avoid them except for quick tests: <p style=\"color: red;\">This text is red.</p> Internal CSS lives in a <style> tag inside the page's <head>. It styles only that one page, which is handy for a single-page test: <head> <style> p { color: blue; } </style> </head> External CSS lives in its own file, usually called styles.css, and the page links to it. This is the right way for a real website — one file styles every page, so changing one line changes the whole site: <head> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"> </head> This website uses external CSS. Every page you have opened so far loads the same styles.css file. When you change the theme from dark to light, one file does all the work.", "w": 153}, {"l": "css-1", "n": 6, "t": "What is CSS", "m": "CSS", "h": "Selectors and rules", "x": "A CSS rule has two parts: a selector that picks which elements to style, and a declaration block in curly braces that says how to style them. Each declaration is a property and a value, separated by a colon, ending with a semicolon: h1 { color: darkblue; font-size: 32px; } That rule says: every <h1> on the page should be dark blue and 32 pixels tall. There are three basic selectors. An element selector (h1, p) styles every element of that type. A class selector starts with a dot (.warning) and styles every element with class=\"warning\" — the same class can be reused on many elements. An ID selector starts with a hash (#main-title) and styles the one element with that id: p { color: #333; } /* every paragraph */ .warning { color: red; } /* every element with class=\"warning\" */ #main-title { color: navy; } /* the one element with id=\"main-title\" */ Use classes for anything you will repeat, and ids for one unique thing on the page. And remember: CSS ignores extra spaces and line breaks, so format your rules neatly — future you will be grateful.", "w": 190}, {"l": "css-1", "n": 6, "t": "What is CSS", "m": "CSS", "h": "Your first styled page", "x": "Let us put it together. This small page has no CSS file yet — the style is internal, so you can try it in one file: <!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>My Styled Page</title> <style> body { background-color: #f9fafb; } h1 { color: #d97706; } p { color: #333333; font-size: 18px; } .highlight { background-color: #fef3c7; } </style> </head> <body> <h1>Hello, styled world!</h1> <p class=\"highlight\">This paragraph has a yellow background because of its class.</p> </body> </html> Change one value — the heading colour, the font size — save, and refresh. That instant feedback is the fun of CSS: you see your change the moment you save.", "w": 105}, {"l": "css-1", "n": 6, "t": "What is CSS", "m": "CSS", "h": "Exercise — Style your personal page", "x": "Create a file called styles.css next to your personal page, and link it with a link tag in the head. In the CSS file, set the body background to a light colour, make the h1 dark blue and larger, and give one paragraph a class called highlight with a yellow background. Refresh your page — the styles should apply without changing any HTML except the one link tag. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 75}, {"l": "css-1", "n": 6, "t": "What is CSS", "m": "CSS", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does CSS stand for? Correct answer: Cascading Style Sheets. Why: CSS is short for Cascading Style Sheets. Question: Which way of adding CSS is best for a whole website? Correct answer: An external .css file linked from every page. Why: One external file styles every page, so one change updates the whole site. Question: What does the selector .menu do? Correct answer: It selects every element with class=\"menu\". Why: A dot in front of a name selects elements by class.", "w": 87}, {"l": "css-2", "n": 7, "t": "Colours, Fonts & Text", "m": "CSS", "h": "Colours in CSS", "x": "CSS gives you three ways to write a colour. Colour names are the simplest — red, darkblue, gold — but there are only 140 of them, and \"lightblue\" may not be the exact blue you want. Hex codes are the professional standard: a hash followed by six digits, two each for red, green and blue. #ff0000 is pure red, #000000 is black, #ffffff is white, and #a88462 is the tan colour this website uses: h1 { color: #6b4a2e; } /* brown heading */ p { color: #333333; } /* dark grey text */ body { background-color: #f9fafb; } /* very light grey */ RGB writes the same three numbers in decimal: rgb(255, 0, 0) is the same red as #ff0000. RGBA adds a fourth number for transparency — rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5) is black at half strength, so whatever is behind it shows through. Use color for text and background-color for the fill behind it. One rule of design: text must be easy to read. Dark text on a light background, or light text on a dark background — never light grey on white.", "w": 184}, {"l": "css-2", "n": 7, "t": "Colours, Fonts & Text", "m": "CSS", "h": "Fonts", "x": "The font-family property picks the typeface. You do not name one font — you name a list, called a font stack, from your first choice to a safe fallback. If the visitor's device does not have your first font, it tries the next one: body { font-family: Georgia, \"Times New Roman\", serif; } h1 { font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; } There are five generic families every device understands: serif (letters with little feet, like Times New Roman — good for long reading), sans-serif (clean letters without feet, like Arial — good for screens), monospace (every letter the same width, like Courier — for code), cursive and fantasy. Always end your stack with a generic family, so the text never falls back to a random font. You can also load web fonts from services like Google Fonts, which gives you thousands of free fonts with one link tag. That is how most modern sites get their distinctive look. For now, system fonts are enough — learn the properties first, then explore fonts later.", "w": 172}, {"l": "css-2", "n": 7, "t": "Colours, Fonts & Text", "m": "CSS", "h": "Text size and weight", "x": "Size is set with font-size. Pixels (px) are exact and easy: 16px is the browser default for body text. But pixels do not scale when a visitor zooms or changes their settings, so professionals often use rem — one rem equals the root font size (usually 16px), so 1.5rem is 24px and it grows if the visitor makes their text bigger: h1 { font-size: 2.5rem; } /* about 40px */ p { font-size: 1rem; } /* 16px, the default */ small { font-size: 0.85rem; } font-weight controls boldness: 400 is normal, 700 is bold, and 300 is light. line-height controls the space between lines — 1.6 (no unit, a multiple of the font size) makes long paragraphs much easier to read than the cramped default of about 1.2.", "w": 128}, {"l": "css-2", "n": 7, "t": "Colours, Fonts & Text", "m": "CSS", "h": "Text alignment and style", "x": "Three more properties finish the basics. text-align moves text left, right, centre or justified (left is the default for English, and the easiest to read — centre is for headings and short lines, never for long paragraphs). text-decoration adds or removes underlines — links are underlined by default, and text-decoration: none is how navigation links lose the line. letter-spacing adds space between letters, which makes small capital-style headings look sharp: h2 { text-align: center; letter-spacing: 1px; } a { text-decoration: none; } a:hover { text-decoration: underline; } That last rule uses a pseudo-class: :hover means \"when the mouse is over this link\". Pseudo-classes let you style an element in a particular state — :hover, :focus (when a field is selected), :first-child. You will use :hover constantly, starting with the mini projects.", "w": 131}, {"l": "css-2", "n": 7, "t": "Colours, Fonts & Text", "m": "CSS", "h": "Exercise — A readable article page", "x": "Make a small article page with one h1, two h2 headings and three paragraphs. Style it: body font Georgia, serif; h1 dark blue at 2.5rem, centred; paragraphs at 1rem with line-height 1.7; links with no underline until hover. Then change the h1 colour to a hex code of your choice and watch it update. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 61}, {"l": "css-2", "n": 7, "t": "Colours, Fonts & Text", "m": "CSS", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which hex code is black? Correct answer: #000000. Why: #000000 means zero red, zero green, zero blue — black. Question: Which property changes the font of an element? Correct answer: font-family. Why: font-family picks the typeface, with fallbacks in a comma-separated list. Question: What is 1rem relative to? Correct answer: The root element's font size. Why: rem is relative to the root (html) font size, usually 16px.", "w": 73}, {"l": "css-3", "n": 8, "t": "Box Model", "m": "CSS", "h": "Four layers around every element", "x": "Every element on a web page is a box. Not a metaphor — a real rectangle, with four layers. From the inside out: the content (the text or image itself), the padding (space inside the box, between the content and the edge), the border (the line around the box), and the margin (space outside the box, between it and its neighbours). Most layout problems in CSS are box model problems. An element looks too big? Probably padding. Two boxes touch each other? Probably a missing margin. Elements are different sizes even though you set the same width? Almost certainly the box model — and the fix is in the last section of this lesson.", "w": 114}, {"l": "css-3", "n": 8, "t": "Box Model", "m": "CSS", "h": "Padding and margin", "x": "padding is the space inside the box. It takes the element's background colour with it — padding on a coloured button is coloured too. margin is the space outside the box. It is always transparent, and it pushes other elements away: .card { padding: 20px; /* 20px on all four sides */ margin: 16px; /* 16px gap around the card */ background: #f3f4f6; } You can set each side separately — padding-top, padding-right, padding-bottom, padding-left — or use shorthand. With two values, the first is top and bottom, the second is left and right. With four values, the order is clockwise from the top: top, right, bottom, left. A handy way to remember it is \"TRouBLe\": Top, Right, Bottom, Left. .box { padding: 10px 20px; } /* 10px top/bottom, 20px left/right */ .box { margin: 8px 12px 8px 12px; } /* top, right, bottom, left */ One quirk: vertical margins between two elements do not add up — they collapse into the larger one. Two paragraphs with margin: 20px have a 20px gap between them, not 40px. This is normal CSS behaviour, not a bug, and once you know it, it stops surprising you.", "w": 194}, {"l": "css-3", "n": 8, "t": "Box Model", "m": "CSS", "h": "Borders", "x": "A border is the line around the padding. You set its width, style and colour, usually in one shorthand line: .box { border: 2px solid #d97706; border-radius: 10px; } The style can be solid, dashed, dotted or none. border-radius rounds the corners — 10px gives soft corners, 50% turns a square into a circle, which is how round profile photos are made. Borders are also how you debug layout: give an element a bright red border and you can see exactly where its box is.", "w": 85}, {"l": "css-3", "n": 8, "t": "Box Model", "m": "CSS", "h": "box-sizing: border-box", "x": "Here is the trap. By default, when you write width: 300px, the browser means \"the content is 300px wide\" — and then it adds the padding and border on top. A box with width: 300px and padding: 20px is actually 340px wide on screen. Two boxes that should fit side by side suddenly do not, and beginners spend hours wondering why. The fix is one rule, and professionals put it at the top of every stylesheet: * { box-sizing: border-box; } With border-box, the width you set includes the padding and the border. A 300px box with 20px padding is 300px wide — the content area shrinks to fit. This website uses this rule, and so should you, in every project from now on. One last useful trick: a block element with a fixed width and margin: 0 auto centres itself on the page. The left and right margins are set to auto, and the browser splits the leftover space equally: .container { width: 90%; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; } That is how this lesson page is centred — a container with a maximum width and automatic side margins.", "w": 190}, {"l": "css-3", "n": 8, "t": "Box Model", "m": "CSS", "h": "Exercise — A card with the box model", "x": "Build a card: a div with a heading and a paragraph inside. Give it a light background, 20px padding, a 2px solid border, 12px rounded corners, and 16px margin. Then add box-sizing: border-box to your stylesheet, set the card width to 320px, and check that it stays 320px wide including its padding. Finally, centre the card with margin: 0 auto. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 67}, {"l": "css-3", "n": 8, "t": "Box Model", "m": "CSS", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which layer of the box model sits between the content and the border? Correct answer: Padding. Why: From the inside out: content, padding, border, margin. Question: What does box-sizing: border-box do? Correct answer: It makes the width include padding and border. Why: With border-box, a 300px box stays 300px wide even with padding and a border. Question: How do you centre a block element that has a fixed width? Correct answer: margin: 0 auto. Why: margin: 0 auto splits the leftover space equally on the left and right.", "w": 94}, {"l": "css-4", "n": 9, "t": "Flexbox Layout", "m": "CSS", "h": "What is Flexbox?", "x": "Before Flexbox, laying out a row of boxes in CSS was painful — floats, hacks, and crossed fingers. Flexbox is the modern answer. It is a one-dimensional layout system: it arranges items in a row or a column, and it handles the spacing and alignment for you. To use it, you set display: flex on a parent element. That parent becomes the flex container, and its direct children become flex items. The items line up in a row by default, and they shrink or grow to fit: .row { display: flex; } .row div { background: #fef3c7; border: 1px solid #d97706; padding: 16px; } With those few lines, three divs sit side by side in a neat row. No floats, no widths to calculate. If you add a fourth div, it joins the row automatically.", "w": 135}, {"l": "css-4", "n": 9, "t": "Flexbox Layout", "m": "CSS", "h": "Direction: row or column", "x": "The flex-direction property sets the direction of the main axis. row is the default — items go left to right. column stacks them top to bottom: .row { display: flex; flex-direction: row; } .column { display: flex; flex-direction: column; } You can also reverse either direction with row-reverse or column-reverse. The direction matters because it decides what the next two properties mean: justify-content always works along the main axis (the direction you chose), and align-items works along the other axis.", "w": 80}, {"l": "css-4", "n": 9, "t": "Flexbox Layout", "m": "CSS", "h": "Aligning items", "x": "justify-content spreads items along the main axis. The values you will use most are flex-start (items at the start), center, flex-end, space-between (first item at the start, last at the end, space spread evenly between) and space-around: .navbar { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; } align-items aligns items along the other axis — for a row, that means vertically. center is the famous one: it vertically centres items, which used to be one of the hardest things in CSS. And gap puts a fixed space between items, so you no longer need margins on every child: .menu { display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: center; } If the items do not fit, they shrink by default. Add flex-wrap: wrap and they move onto a new line instead of squeezing — useful for a grid of cards on a small phone screen.", "w": 141}, {"l": "css-4", "n": 9, "t": "Flexbox Layout", "m": "CSS", "h": "A navbar with Flexbox", "x": "Here is the pattern behind almost every website header: a logo on the left, links on the right, everything vertically centred. Three rules do the whole job: <header class=\"navbar\"> <a class=\"logo\" href=\"#\">My Site</a> <nav> <a href=\"#\">Home</a> <a href=\"#\">About</a> <a href=\"#\">Contact</a> </nav> </header> .navbar { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: 12px 20px; background: #111827; } .navbar a { color: white; text-decoration: none; } .navbar nav { display: flex; gap: 16px; } The header is a flex container, so the logo and the nav sit at opposite ends. The nav is itself a flex container, so its links sit in a row with a 16px gap. Nested flex containers like this are how real layouts are built — small rows inside bigger rows. You will use exactly this pattern in Mini Project 3.", "w": 133}, {"l": "css-4", "n": 9, "t": "Flexbox Layout", "m": "CSS", "h": "Exercise — A row of three cards", "x": "Make a page with three cards (each a div with a heading and a sentence). Put them inside a container with display: flex and a 16px gap. Then try: change justify-content to center, then to space-between, and watch the cards move. Finally, add flex-wrap: wrap and shrink your browser window — the cards should drop onto a new line instead of squeezing. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 69}, {"l": "css-4", "n": 9, "t": "Flexbox Layout", "m": "CSS", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which property turns an element into a flex container? Correct answer: display: flex. Why: display: flex makes the element a flex container and its children flex items. Question: justify-content aligns items along which axis? Correct answer: The main axis. Why: justify-content works along the main axis; align-items works along the cross axis. Question: What is the default flex-direction? Correct answer: row. Why: The default is row — items line up left to right.", "w": 79}, {"l": "css-5", "n": 10, "t": "Responsive Design", "m": "CSS", "h": "Why responsive?", "x": "More than half of all web traffic comes from phones. A website that only looks right on a laptop is a website that fails most of its visitors. Responsive design means one website that adapts to any screen: the same HTML, with CSS that changes the layout as the screen gets smaller. You have already seen it on this site. Open this lesson on your phone: the navigation collapses into a menu button, the text stays readable, and nothing runs off the edge of the screen. On a laptop, the same page spreads out wider. One file, two experiences — that is responsive design.", "w": 104}, {"l": "css-5", "n": 10, "t": "Responsive Design", "m": "CSS", "h": "The viewport tag", "x": "Every responsive page starts with one line in the <head>. Without it, phones pretend to be a 980px-wide desktop screen and shrink the whole page down to fit — tiny text, and the visitor has to pinch and zoom: <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"> This tag tells the phone: \"the page width is the device width, and start at normal zoom\". With it, your CSS sees the real screen size — 390px on a phone, 1280px on a laptop — and can respond to it. Every page in this course has this tag. Check any page you build from now on: if it looks tiny on your phone, the viewport tag is the first thing to check.", "w": 116}, {"l": "css-5", "n": 10, "t": "Responsive Design", "m": "CSS", "h": "Media queries", "x": "A media query is a CSS rule that only applies when the screen matches a condition — usually its width. It is how you say \"on small screens, do this differently\": /* Default: three cards in a row */ .cards { display: flex; gap: 16px; } /* On screens 700px wide or smaller: stack them */ @media (max-width: 700px) { .cards { flex-direction: column; } h1 { font-size: 1.8rem; } } The rules inside the media query override the normal rules, but only when the condition is true. max-width: 700px means \"up to 700px wide\" — phones and small tablets. You can also use min-width for \"700px and wider\". The number you choose is called a breakpoint, and 700px is a good first one: below it, you are probably on a phone.", "w": 132}, {"l": "css-5", "n": 10, "t": "Responsive Design", "m": "CSS", "h": "Flexible layouts", "x": "Media queries handle the big changes. For the small ones, use flexible sizes from the start. Percentages grow and shrink with their parent: width: 100% fills the available space, whatever it is. max-width sets a ceiling — max-width: 800px means \"800px on a big screen, less on a small one\". Together they make a container that is wide on a laptop and full-width on a phone, with no media query needed: .container { width: 90%; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; } Images need one rule too. An image with a fixed width of 600px will overflow a 390px phone screen and force horizontal scrolling — the most common responsive bug. This fixes it: img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } The image never gets wider than its container, and height: auto keeps its proportions. Finally, the mobile-first approach: write the CSS for the phone first, then add media queries for bigger screens with min-width. It is easier to add space as the screen grows than to squeeze a desktop design onto a phone — and since most of your visitors are on phones, the phone layout is the one that matters most.", "w": 192}, {"l": "css-5", "n": 10, "t": "Responsive Design", "m": "CSS", "h": "Exercise — Make your page responsive", "x": "Take your personal page and make it responsive: add the viewport meta tag if it is missing, set img max-width: 100%, and add a media query at max-width: 700px that reduces the h1 font size and stacks any row of cards into a column. Test it by shrinking your browser window — the layout should change smoothly, with no horizontal scrolling at any size. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 71}, {"l": "css-5", "n": 10, "t": "Responsive Design", "m": "CSS", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does the viewport meta tag do? Correct answer: It tells the phone the page width matches the device width. Why: Without it, phones pretend to be 980px wide and shrink the page. Question: Which is a correct media query for small screens? Correct answer: @media (max-width: 700px). Why: @media (max-width: 700px) applies its rules only up to 700px wide. Question: Why set max-width: 100% on images? Correct answer: So images never overflow a small screen. Why: A fixed-width image can overflow a 390px phone screen; max-width: 100% stops that.", "w": 96}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "What is HTML?", "x": "HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. It is the language that builds the structure of every web page. When you open a website, the browser reads the HTML file and turns it into the page you see — the headings, the text, the images, the buttons. HTML is not a programming language like Python or JavaScript. You do not write logic or calculations in HTML. You write markup: you tell the browser what each piece of content is. This is a heading. This is a paragraph. This is a link. The browser then displays each piece in the right way. Think of a web page like a house. HTML is the walls, the rooms and the doors — the structure. CSS is the paint and the furniture — the style. JavaScript is the electricity and the switches — the behaviour. In this course you learn all three, but we start with the structure, because nothing else works without it.", "w": 159}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "Tags and elements", "x": "HTML is written with tags. A tag is a word inside angle brackets, like <p>. Most tags come in pairs: an opening tag and a closing tag. The closing tag has a forward slash, like </p>. The text between the two tags is the content. Together, the opening tag, the content and the closing tag form an element. Here is the simplest element in HTML — a paragraph: <p>Hello, world!</p> A few tags do not need a closing tag. They are called empty elements. The line break <br> and the image tag <img> are two examples. You will meet them in later lessons. Tags can also carry extra information called attributes. An attribute sits inside the opening tag, like this: <a href=\"https://example.com\">. The href attribute tells the link where to go. You will use attributes a lot, starting from Lesson 2.", "w": 141}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "The basic structure of a page", "x": "Every HTML page has the same skeleton. It looks small, but every website on the internet — from a personal blog to a big online store — starts from this shape: <!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>My First Page</title> </head> <body> <h1>Hello, world!</h1> <p>This is my first web page.</p> </body> </html> Let us read it line by line. <!DOCTYPE html> tells the browser this is a modern HTML document. <html> is the root element — everything on the page lives inside it. The <head> holds information about the page that visitors do not see, like the page title shown in the browser tab. The <body> holds everything visitors do see: headings, text, images and links. The <h1> tag is the main heading of the page. The <p> tag is a paragraph. That is all you need for your first page.", "w": 139}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "How the browser reads your page", "x": "When you open an HTML file in a browser, the browser reads it from top to bottom and builds the page. It ignores extra spaces and line breaks in your code, so you can format your code neatly without changing how the page looks. Indentation — the spaces at the start of a line — is for humans, not for the browser. Good indentation makes your code easy to read, so keep it tidy from day one. One more thing: HTML is forgiving. If you forget a closing tag, the browser will usually guess what you meant and still show the page. Do not rely on this. Browsers guess differently, and a page that looks fine in Chrome can look broken in another browser. Close your tags, always.", "w": 128}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "Common beginner mistakes", "x": "Forgetting the closing tag, like writing <p> without </p>. Mixing up the order of closing tags. Tags must close in reverse order: <p><strong>text</strong></p>, never <p><strong>text</p></strong>. Putting visible content inside <head> instead of <body>. Nothing in the head shows on the page. Using capital letters in tag names. HTML works with capitals, but the standard is lowercase — write <p>, not <P>.", "w": 61}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "Exercise — Build your first page", "x": "Open a text editor (Notepad on Windows, TextEdit on Mac, or VS Code if you have it). Type the full page from the example above, but change the heading to your name and the paragraph to one sentence about yourself. Save the file as my-first-page.html and open it in your browser. You just built your first web page — and that is exactly how every web developer started.", "w": 68}, {"l": "html-1", "n": 1, "t": "Your First Web Page", "m": "HTML", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does HTML stand for? Correct answer: HyperText Markup Language. Why: HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language — it builds the structure of every web page. Question: Which tag is the main heading of a page? Correct answer: <h1>. Why: The <h1> tag is the main heading of the page. Question: What goes inside the <head> of a page? Correct answer: Information about the page, like the title. Why: The <head> holds information about the page that visitors do not see, like the title in the browser tab.", "w": 94}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Headings: h1 to h6", "x": "Headings organise your page the way chapter titles organise a book. HTML gives you six levels, from <h1> (the biggest, most important) to <h6> (the smallest). Browsers show them in decreasing size by default, but size is not their real job — their real job is meaning. Search engines and screen readers use headings to understand what your page is about. <h1>My Travel Blog</h1> <h2>Places I Visited in 2026</h2> <h3>Lahore</h3> <h3>Hunza</h3> <h2>What I Packed</h2> Notice the pattern: one <h1> for the whole page, then <h2> for the big sections, then <h3> for smaller parts inside those sections. Never skip levels — do not jump from <h1> straight to <h4> — and never use a heading only because you want big bold text. If something is not a heading, use a paragraph and style it with CSS later.", "w": 137}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Paragraphs: the p tag", "x": "Most of the text on the internet lives inside <p> tags. A paragraph is a block of text, and the browser automatically adds space above and below it. One idea, one paragraph — that is the rule for readable writing on the web. <p>HTML is the structure of every web page. It tells the browser what each piece of content is: a heading, a paragraph, an image, or a link.</p> <p>This is a second paragraph. The browser puts space between the two paragraphs, even though there is only one blank line between them in the code.</p> Inside a paragraph you can use small inline tags. <strong> makes text bold and important, <em> makes text emphasised (italic), and <br> forces a line break inside the same paragraph. Use <br> sparingly — for an address or a poem, not for spacing between paragraphs. That is what the paragraph tag is for.", "w": 149}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Links: the a tag", "x": "The link is what makes the web a web. Without links, every page would be an island. The anchor tag <a> creates a link, and its href attribute says where the link goes: <a href=\"https://example.com\">Visit Example</a> There are two kinds of links. An absolute link has the full address, starting with https://, and it can point to any website in the world. A relative link points to another page on your own site, using only the file path — no domain name needed: <!-- Absolute link: goes to another website --> <a href=\"https://www.wikipedia.org\">Wikipedia</a> <!-- Relative link: goes to a page on your own site --> <a href=\"about.html\">About Me</a> <a href=\"lessons/html-1.html\">Lesson 1</a> Relative links are the right choice inside your own website, because they keep working even if your domain name changes. Absolute links are for other people's websites. By default, a link opens in the same browser tab. If you link to another website, it is polite to open it in a new tab so your visitor does not lose your page. Add target=\"_blank\" for that: <a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\"> Open Example in a new tab </a> One last rule about links: the link text should say where the link goes. \"Click here\" tells the reader nothing. \"Read Lesson 1\" tells them everything. Good link text helps your readers, and it helps search engines understand your site too.", "w": 227}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Linking to a part of the same page", "x": "Sometimes you want a link that jumps to a section on the same page — like a \"Back to top\" link, or a table of contents at the start of a long article. You do it in two steps. First, give the section an id attribute. An id is a unique name for one element on the page. Second, link to it with a hash (#) in front of the name: <!-- Step 1: give the section an id --> <h2 id=\"menu\">Our Menu</h2> <!-- Step 2: link to it with a hash --> <a href=\"#menu\">Jump to the menu</a> When the reader clicks the link, the browser scrolls straight to the section with that id. The id must be unique — two elements on the same page must never share an id — and it cannot contain spaces, so use hyphens, like id=\"my-favourite-books\". You will use ids again in the CSS and JavaScript modules, where they let you style and control one exact element.", "w": 163}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Putting it together", "x": "Headings, paragraphs and links are the three tags you will use more than any others. A complete little page needs nothing else: <h1>Ali's Bakery</h1> <p>Fresh bread, cakes and biscuits, baked every morning in Lahore.</p> <h2>Our Menu</h2> <p>See the full menu on our <a href=\"menu.html\">menu page</a>, or visit our <a href=\"https://instagram.com\" target=\"_blank\">Instagram page</a> for daily photos.</p>", "w": 55}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Exercise — A page about yourself", "x": "Open your my-first-page.html file from Lesson 1 and add: an <h1> with your name, an <h2> called \"About Me\", two paragraphs about yourself, an <h2> called \"My Favourite Websites\", and three links to real websites you like (open them in new tabs). Save and refresh your browser. You now have a real personal page with structure, text and links.", "w": 59}, {"l": "html-2", "n": 2, "t": "Headings, Paragraphs & Links", "m": "HTML", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: How many heading levels does HTML have? Correct answer: Six. Why: HTML gives you six heading levels, from <h1> (biggest) to <h6> (smallest). Question: Which attribute says where a link goes? Correct answer: href. Why: The href attribute of the <a> tag says where the link goes. Question: How do you link to a section on the same page? Correct answer: Give the section an id and link to #id. Why: Give the section an id, then link to it with a hash in front of the name, like #menu.", "w": 96}, {"l": "html-3", "n": 3, "t": "Images, Lists & Tables", "m": "HTML", "h": "Images: the img tag", "x": "Text alone makes a dull page. Images make it come alive. The <img> tag adds a picture to your page. It is an empty element — it has no closing tag — and it needs two attributes to do its job: <img src=\"cat.jpg\" alt=\"A grey cat sleeping in the sun\"> The src attribute (short for \"source\") says where the image file is. It can be a file in the same folder as your page (cat.jpg), a file in another folder (images/cat.jpg), or a full web address (https://example.com/cat.jpg). The alt attribute (short for \"alternative text\") describes the picture in words. The alt text matters more than beginners think. If the image fails to load, the browser shows the alt text instead. Screen readers read it out loud for blind visitors. Search engines use it to understand what the picture shows. Every image needs an alt attribute. If an image is purely decorative, use an empty alt (alt=\"\") so screen readers skip it — but never leave the attribute out completely. You can also control the size with the width and height attributes, measured in pixels: <img src=\"team.jpg\" alt=\"Our team at the Lahore office\" width=\"600\" height=\"400\"> Setting both width and height also stops the page from jumping around while the image loads, which makes your site feel faster. In the CSS module you will learn a better way to size images, but these attributes are a good start.", "w": 236}, {"l": "html-3", "n": 3, "t": "Images, Lists & Tables", "m": "HTML", "h": "Lists: ul, ol and li", "x": "Lists are everywhere on the web: navigation menus, shopping lists, steps in a recipe, features on a pricing page. HTML has two kinds. An unordered list (<ul>) is for items with no special order. The browser shows a bullet point in front of each item: <ul> <li>Fresh bread</li> <li>Chocolate cake</li> <li>Butter biscuits</li> </ul> An ordered list (<ol>) is for items where the order matters — steps, rankings, instructions. The browser numbers each item automatically: <ol> <li>Mix the flour and sugar.</li> <li>Add the eggs and milk.</li> <li>Bake for 30 minutes.</li> </ol> Every item in both kinds of list uses the <li> tag. You can also nest lists — put a whole <ul> inside one <li> — to make a list inside a list. That is how multi-level navigation menus are built.", "w": 130}, {"l": "html-3", "n": 3, "t": "Images, Lists & Tables", "m": "HTML", "h": "Tables: rows and cells", "x": "Tables are for data that belongs in rows and columns: a class timetable, a price list, match scores. A table is built from three tags. <table> is the whole table. <tr> is a table row. Inside each row, <th> is a header cell (bold, centred) and <td> is a normal data cell: <table> <tr> <th>Day</th> <th>Class</th> <th>Time</th> </tr> <tr> <td>Monday</td> <td>HTML Basics</td> <td>9:00 AM</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Wednesday</td> <td>CSS Basics</td> <td>9:00 AM</td> </tr> </table> Read the code like a grid: each <tr> is one horizontal line, and the cells inside it are the columns. The first row uses <th> because it is the header — Day, Class, Time. Every row after that uses <td>. One warning: tables are only for tabular data. In the early days of the web, people used tables to lay out whole pages, and that was a bad idea — it makes pages slow, hard to change and impossible to read on a phone. For layout, use CSS (you will learn Flexbox in the CSS module). For data, use tables.", "w": 173}, {"l": "html-3", "n": 3, "t": "Images, Lists & Tables", "m": "HTML", "h": "Putting it together", "x": "Images, lists and tables often appear together on one page. A shop page might show a product image, a list of features, and a table of prices. You now know all three tags, so you can build that page already.", "w": 40}, {"l": "html-3", "n": 3, "t": "Images, Lists & Tables", "m": "HTML", "h": "Exercise — Your favourites page", "x": "Add a new section to your personal page from Lesson 2: an <h2> called \"My Favourite Foods\", an unordered list of three foods, an <h2> called \"My Weekly Schedule\", and a table with three rows (Day, Activity, Time). Then add one image — any picture saved in the same folder as your page — with a proper alt description. Save, refresh, and check that everything shows correctly. If the image does not appear, check the file name in src — one wrong letter is the most common cause.", "w": 88}, {"l": "html-3", "n": 3, "t": "Images, Lists & Tables", "m": "HTML", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which two attributes does every <img> tag need? Correct answer: src and alt. Why: src says where the image file is; alt describes the picture in words. Question: Which tag makes an ordered (numbered) list? Correct answer: <ol>. Why: <ol> is an ordered list — the browser numbers each item automatically. Question: In a table, which tag is a header cell? Correct answer: <th>. Why: <th> is a header cell (bold, centred); <td> is a normal data cell.", "w": 84}, {"l": "html-4", "n": 4, "t": "Forms & Inputs", "m": "HTML", "h": "What is a form?", "x": "A form is how a website collects information from a visitor: a login box, a signup page, a contact form, a search bar. Without forms, a website can only talk at you. With forms, it can listen. A form is built with the <form> tag. Everything the visitor fills in goes inside it. Two attributes matter most. The action attribute says where the data goes when the form is submitted — usually a page or a server address. The method attribute says how it is sent: get puts the data in the web address (good for a search form), post sends it hidden in the request (good for logins and signups, because passwords should never appear in the address bar). <form action=\"/signup\" method=\"post\"> <!-- inputs go here --> </form> In this course the form does not send data anywhere yet — there is no server in version 1. That is fine. You are learning the structure now; in the JavaScript module you will make the form respond on the page itself.", "w": 171}, {"l": "html-4", "n": 4, "t": "Forms & Inputs", "m": "HTML", "h": "Text inputs", "x": "The <input> tag is the workhorse of every form. It is an empty element, and its type attribute decides what kind of field it becomes: <input type=\"text\" placeholder=\"Your name\"> <input type=\"email\" placeholder=\"you@example.com\"> <input type=\"password\" placeholder=\"Choose a password\"> <input type=\"number\" min=\"1\" max=\"100\"> <input type=\"date\"> The type does more than change the look. type=\"email\" makes the browser check that the text looks like an email address. type=\"password\" hides the letters as dots. type=\"number\" shows up and down arrows on desktop and a number keyboard on phones. On a phone, the right keyboard appears automatically — that is a small detail your visitors will notice. Two more attributes you will use constantly: placeholder shows a grey hint inside an empty field, and required stops the form from submitting until the field is filled. Always pair an input with a <label>. The label's for attribute must match the input's id — then clicking the label focuses the field, and screen readers can read the form out loud: <label for=\"email\">Email address</label> <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required> The name attribute is the field's name in the data that gets sent. Without a name, the field's value is never sent at all — a common beginner bug.", "w": 200}, {"l": "html-4", "n": 4, "t": "Forms & Inputs", "m": "HTML", "h": "Choices: checkboxes, radios and dropdowns", "x": "Not every answer is typed. Sometimes the visitor picks from choices. A checkbox is for yes-or-no questions and for picking several items from a list: <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"topics\" value=\"html\"> HTML</label> <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"topics\" value=\"css\"> CSS</label> Radio buttons are for picking exactly one option from a group. All the radios in one group share the same name — that is what links them together, so choosing one un-chooses the others: <label><input type=\"radio\" name=\"plan\" value=\"free\"> Free</label> <label><input type=\"radio\" name=\"plan\" value=\"paid\"> Paid</label> For a long list of options, a dropdown saves space. The <select> tag holds the list, and each choice is an <option>: <label for=\"city\">City</label> <select id=\"city\" name=\"city\"> <option value=\"lahore\">Lahore</option> <option value=\"karachi\">Karachi</option> <option value=\"islamabad\">Islamabad</option> </select> The value is what gets sent; the text between the option tags is what the visitor sees. They can be different — for example, value=\"pk\" with the text \"Pakistan\".", "w": 142}, {"l": "html-4", "n": 4, "t": "Forms & Inputs", "m": "HTML", "h": "Text areas and the submit button", "x": "For longer text — a message, a comment, a description — use <textarea>. Unlike <input>, it has an opening and a closing tag, and the rows attribute sets how tall it is: <label for=\"message\">Your message</label> <textarea id=\"message\" name=\"message\" rows=\"4\"></textarea> Finally, the form needs a way to send. A <button type=\"submit\"> inside the form submits it. A <button type=\"reset\"> clears all the fields — use it rarely, because visitors click it by accident and lose their work. A plain <button type=\"button\"> does nothing on its own; JavaScript gives it a job later. <button type=\"submit\">Send message</button> Here is a complete contact form, putting everything together: <form action=\"/contact\" method=\"post\"> <label for=\"name\">Name</label> <input type=\"text\" id=\"name\" name=\"name\" required> <label for=\"email\">Email</label> <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required> <label for=\"message\">Message</label> <textarea id=\"message\" name=\"message\" rows=\"4\"></textarea> <button type=\"submit\">Send message</button> </form>", "w": 130}, {"l": "html-4", "n": 4, "t": "Forms & Inputs", "m": "HTML", "h": "Exercise — A signup form", "x": "Add a signup form to your personal page: a text input for your name, an email input, a password input, a dropdown for your city, one checkbox for 'Send me updates', and a submit button. Make every field required except the checkbox, and give every input a label with a matching for and id. Open the page and try submitting it empty — the browser should stop you and point at the first empty field. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 82}, {"l": "html-4", "n": 4, "t": "Forms & Inputs", "m": "HTML", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which input type should you use for an email address? Correct answer: type=\"email\". Why: type=\"email\" makes the browser check that the text looks like an email address. Question: What does the label tag's for attribute do? Correct answer: It links the label to an input by its id. Why: for must match the input's id — then clicking the label focuses the field. Question: Which attribute stops a form from submitting until a field is filled? Correct answer: required. Why: required tells the browser the field must be filled before the form can submit.", "w": 100}, {"l": "html-5", "n": 5, "t": "Semantic HTML & Page Structure", "m": "HTML", "h": "What does semantic mean?", "x": "A semantic tag is one whose name says what the content is, not just how it looks. <div> says nothing — it is a generic box. <nav> says \"this is navigation\". <article> says \"this is a self-contained piece of content\". The browser shows them all the same by default. The difference is for everyone else who reads your page: screen readers, search engines, and other developers. Think of it like labelling boxes when you move house. A box marked \"kitchen\" is useful. A box marked \"stuff\" is not. Semantic tags are labels on your page. A blind visitor using a screen reader can jump straight to the <main> content and skip the navigation. Google understands that the text inside <article> is the real content of the page, and ranks it better. And when you come back to your own code in six months, <footer> tells you instantly what that part is.", "w": 151}, {"l": "html-5", "n": 5, "t": "Semantic HTML & Page Structure", "m": "HTML", "h": "The main page tags", "x": "Almost every page on the web is built from the same five tags: <header> <!-- top of the page: logo, site title --> <nav> <!-- navigation links --> <main> <!-- the main content — only one per page --> <footer> <!-- bottom of the page: copyright, links --> The <header> usually holds the logo and the site name. The <nav> holds the navigation links — it often sits inside the header, but it can also stand on its own. The <main> holds the content that makes this page different from every other page on the site. There must be exactly one <main> per page. The <footer> holds the copyright line, contact links and anything else that belongs at the bottom. Notice that none of these tags does any styling. They are structure, not appearance. CSS decides how they look — you will style them in the next module.", "w": 148}, {"l": "html-5", "n": 5, "t": "Semantic HTML & Page Structure", "m": "HTML", "h": "Sections and articles", "x": "Inside <main>, two more tags organise the content. A <section> groups related content under one heading — a chapter of the page. An <article> is a piece of content that makes sense on its own: a blog post, a news story, a product card. An article can live inside a section, and a section can live inside an article — the rule is about meaning, not nesting. Two smaller tags complete the set. <aside> is for content related to the page but not part of the main flow — a sidebar, a pull-quote, a box of related links. <figure> wraps an image (or a diagram) together with its caption in <figcaption>: <figure> <img src=\"hunza.jpg\" alt=\"Mountains in Hunza valley\"> <figcaption>Hunza valley in autumn.</figcaption> </figure> That is better than a bare <img> followed by a paragraph, because the caption is now clearly attached to the picture — for the browser, for screen readers, and for you.", "w": 154}, {"l": "html-5", "n": 5, "t": "Semantic HTML & Page Structure", "m": "HTML", "h": "Building a page skeleton", "x": "Here is the skeleton of a complete page, using everything in this lesson. Read it top to bottom — you can already see the shape of the whole site: <!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Ali's Bakery</title> </head> <body> <header> <h1>Ali's Bakery</h1> <nav> <a href=\"index.html\">Home</a> <a href=\"menu.html\">Menu</a> <a href=\"contact.html\">Contact</a> </nav> </header> <main> <section> <h2>Fresh every morning</h2> <p>Bread, cakes and biscuits, baked daily.</p> </section> <article> <h2>Today's special</h2> <p>Chocolate cake — Rs 500 a slice.</p> </article> </main> <footer> <p>Ali's Bakery, Lahore — since 2015.</p> </footer> </body> </html> That is the end of the HTML module. You now know the structure of every web page: headings, text, links, images, lists, tables, forms and semantic sections. From the next lesson, you learn to make it beautiful with CSS.", "w": 122}, {"l": "html-5", "n": 5, "t": "Semantic HTML & Page Structure", "m": "HTML", "h": "Exercise — Rebuild your page semantically", "x": "Take your personal page from the earlier lessons and rebuild its structure with semantic tags: wrap the top in a header with a nav, the middle in main with one or two sections, and the bottom in a footer. Wrap your photo in a figure with a figcaption. The page should look exactly the same as before — semantic tags change the meaning, not the appearance. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 73}, {"l": "html-5", "n": 5, "t": "Semantic HTML & Page Structure", "m": "HTML", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which tag holds the main content of a page? Correct answer: <main>. Why: There is exactly one <main> per page, holding the content that makes the page unique. Question: Which tag is for navigation links? Correct answer: <nav>. Why: <nav> marks the navigation block, so screen readers can jump to it. Question: Why use semantic tags instead of divs everywhere? Correct answer: Screen readers and search engines understand the page better. Why: Semantic tags describe meaning — search engines and screen readers use that meaning.", "w": 91}, {"l": "js-1", "n": 11, "t": "What is JavaScript", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "What JavaScript does", "x": "HTML builds the structure of a page. CSS makes it look good. JavaScript makes it do things. It is the programming language of the web: it reacts to clicks, checks form inputs, loads new data without reloading the page, and changes what is on the screen while the visitor watches. Every interactive thing you have used on the web — a like button, a shopping cart, a live search box, a photo slider — is JavaScript. It runs in the visitor's browser, on their device, the moment the page loads. Unlike HTML and CSS, JavaScript is a real programming language: it has variables, conditions, loops and functions, and you can build almost anything with it.", "w": 115}, {"l": "js-1", "n": 11, "t": "What is JavaScript", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Adding JavaScript to a page", "x": "JavaScript lives inside <script> tags. You can put them in the <head>, but the usual place is at the end of the <body> — that way the HTML loads first, and the script runs after the page exists: <body> <h1>Hello</h1> <script> alert(\"Hello from JavaScript!\"); </script> </body> For a real website, the script goes in its own file, just like CSS. A file called script.js is linked with a src attribute: <script src=\"script.js\"></script> This website does exactly that — every page you have opened loads the same script.js, which powers the theme toggle, the progress bar and the quizzes. One script file, shared by the whole site, just like the stylesheet.", "w": 110}, {"l": "js-1", "n": 11, "t": "What is JavaScript", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Your first line: console.log", "x": "The simplest JavaScript command is console.log(). It prints a message to the browser's console — a panel for developers. Open it with F12 (or Ctrl+Shift+J on Windows, Cmd+Option+J on Mac) and click the Console tab: console.log(\"Hello, world!\"); console.log(2 + 3); The first line prints the text. The second prints 5, because JavaScript can do maths. The console is where you will test ideas and find mistakes for the rest of this module — error messages appear there, with the line number where the problem is. When something does not work and the page looks fine, the console is the first place to look. One more thing: every JavaScript statement usually ends with a semicolon. JavaScript will often guess where a statement ends without one, but relying on guessing causes strange bugs. End your statements with semicolons from day one.", "w": 139}, {"l": "js-1", "n": 11, "t": "What is JavaScript", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Making a button do something", "x": "Here is the classic first program in JavaScript: a button that counts clicks. The HTML gives the button an id, and the script finds it and listens for clicks: <button id=\"countBtn\">Clicked 0 times</button> <script> var count = 0; var button = document.getElementById(\"countBtn\"); button.addEventListener(\"click\", function () { count = count + 1; button.textContent = \"Clicked \" + count + \" times\"; }); </script> Do not worry if every line is not clear yet — the next three lessons explain variables, functions and events one by one. The point is the shape: HTML provides the button, JavaScript finds it, listens, and changes it. That pattern — find, listen, change — is the heart of everything you will build in this module.", "w": 119}, {"l": "js-1", "n": 11, "t": "What is JavaScript", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — A greeting button", "x": "Make a page with a button that says 'Say hello'. Add a script at the end of the body that finds the button by its id and, when clicked, shows an alert saying 'Hello, and welcome to my page!'. Open the page, open the console with F12, and add a console.log line that prints 'Button clicked' — watch it appear in the console every time you click. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 74}, {"l": "js-1", "n": 11, "t": "What is JavaScript", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What is JavaScript mainly used for? Correct answer: Behaviour and interactivity. Why: JavaScript makes pages do things: react to clicks, check forms, load data. Question: Where do you usually write JavaScript in an HTML file? Correct answer: Inside <script> tags. Why: JavaScript goes in <script> tags, usually at the end of the body. Question: What does console.log() do? Correct answer: It prints a message to the browser console. Why: console.log() prints to the developer console, opened with F12.", "w": 84}, {"l": "js-2", "n": 12, "t": "Variables & Data", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Storing values", "x": "A variable is a named box that holds a value. You create one with let or const, give it a name, and store something in it with =: let age = 20; const city = \"Lahore\"; let score = 0; let is for values that can change — a score that goes up, a name the visitor edits. const is for values that never change — a city name, a price, a maximum number of seats. If you try to change a const, JavaScript stops you with an error, which is exactly the point: it protects you from changing something by accident. There is also an older keyword, var. You will see it in old code and old tutorials, but modern JavaScript uses let and const — they are safer, because they only exist inside the block where they were created. This course uses let and const only.", "w": 148}, {"l": "js-2", "n": 12, "t": "Variables & Data", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Data types", "x": "Every value in JavaScript has a type. The five you will use every day are: let name = \"Usman\"; // string — text, in quotes let age = 20; // number — no quotes let isStudent = true; // boolean — true or false let middleName = null; // null — deliberately empty let nickname; // undefined — never given a value Strings use single or double quotes — pick one and be consistent. Numbers have no quotes: 20 is a number, \"20\" is a string, and JavaScript treats them very differently (you will see why in a moment). Booleans are only ever true or false — they are the answers to yes-or-no questions, like \"is the user logged in?\" When you are unsure of a value's type, ask with typeof: typeof \"Usman\"; // \"string\" typeof 20; // \"number\" typeof true; // \"boolean\"", "w": 143}, {"l": "js-2", "n": 12, "t": "Variables & Data", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Working with values", "x": "Variables are only useful if you can do things with them. The maths operators are the ones you know: +, -, *, /. And ++ adds one — score++ is short for score = score + 1: let price = 500; let quantity = 3; let total = price * quantity; // 1500 total = total + 100; // 1600 Here is the trap with +: when one side is a string, JavaScript joins the values instead of adding them. 5 + \"5\" is \"55\", not 10. That is why form inputs — which are always strings — need converting with Number() before you do maths with them: let typed = \"20\"; Number(typed) + 5; // 25, a real number Template literals make it easy to build sentences from variables. They use backticks (`) instead of quotes, and ${ } drops a value into the text: let name = \"Usman\"; let age = 20; console.log(`My name is ${name} and I am ${age} years old.`); // My name is Usman and I am 20 years old.", "w": 175}, {"l": "js-2", "n": 12, "t": "Variables & Data", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Naming variables", "x": "Good names make your code readable. The rules are simple: a name can contain letters, digits, $ and _, but it cannot start with a digit, and it cannot contain spaces or hyphens. By convention, JavaScript names use camelCase — first word lowercase, each later word capitalised: studentName, totalPrice, isLoggedIn. Names should say what the value is. x tells you nothing; studentCount tells you everything. Some words are reserved by the language — you cannot name a variable let, function or return — but the browser will tell you if you try.", "w": 92}, {"l": "js-2", "n": 12, "t": "Variables & Data", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — A bill calculator", "x": "Write a script that stores a price (Rs 500) and a quantity (4) in variables, calculates the total, and prints a sentence with a template literal: 'Total: Rs 2000 for 4 items.' Then change the quantity to 2, recalculate, and print the new total. Try printing price + '5' and see what happens — then fix it with Number(). Try this code in the editor →", "w": 66}, {"l": "js-2", "n": 12, "t": "Variables & Data", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which keyword do you use for a value that never changes? Correct answer: const. Why: const is for values that never change; trying to change one is an error. Question: What is typeof \"5\" in JavaScript? Correct answer: \"string\". Why: Quotes make it a string, even though the character is a digit. Question: What is 5 + \"5\" in JavaScript? Correct answer: \"55\". Why: When one side is a string, + joins the values instead of adding them.", "w": 84}, {"l": "js-3", "n": 13, "t": "Functions", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "What is a function?", "x": "A function is a named block of code that does one job — and you can run it as many times as you like. Instead of writing the same five lines every time you need a total, you write them once inside a function, give it a name, and call it whenever you need it: function greet() { console.log(\"Hello, welcome to WebDev Lab!\"); } greet(); // runs the function greet(); // runs it again Functions are the single most important idea in programming. They keep your code short, they give a name to a job so the code reads like a story, and when something is wrong, there is exactly one place to fix it.", "w": 115}, {"l": "js-3", "n": 13, "t": "Functions", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Parameters and return values", "x": "Most functions need input. A parameter is a placeholder in the function's definition — a name for the value it will receive. An argument is the actual value you pass when you call it: function add(a, b) { return a + b; } let result = add(3, 4); // result is 7 Here a and b are parameters, and 3 and 4 are arguments. The return statement sends a value back to the caller and ends the function — nothing after a return runs. A function without return still runs its code, but it gives back undefined. A function can take any number of parameters, and a parameter can have a default value that applies when the caller leaves it out: function greet(name = \"friend\") { return \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\"; } greet(\"Usman\"); // \"Hello, Usman!\" greet(); // \"Hello, friend!\"", "w": 142}, {"l": "js-3", "n": 13, "t": "Functions", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Calling functions", "x": "Calling a function is just writing its name followed by parentheses. The value it returns can be stored in a variable, printed, or passed into another function — functions are values too, in the sense that their results are values you can use anywhere: function square(n) { return n * n; } let area = square(5); // 25 console.log(square(area)); // 625 Be careful with one common mistake: greet without parentheses is the function itself, while greet() with parentheses runs it. When you hand a function to an event listener (next lesson), you pass it without parentheses — button.addEventListener(\"click\", greet) — because you want the browser to call it later, not right now.", "w": 112}, {"l": "js-3", "n": 13, "t": "Functions", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Arrow functions", "x": "There is a shorter way to write a function, called an arrow function. It does the same job with less typing, and you will see it everywhere in modern code: const add = (a, b) => { return a + b; }; const double = (n) => n * 2; // one line: return is implied When an arrow function has only one line, you can drop the curly braces and the return — the value is returned automatically. Arrow functions are especially common for short jobs passed to other functions, like event listeners and array methods. Use the normal function style while you are learning, and switch to arrows when they feel natural.", "w": 114}, {"l": "js-3", "n": 13, "t": "Functions", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — A price calculator function", "x": "Write a function called totalPrice that takes a price and a quantity, returns price times quantity, and test it with (500, 3) and (1200, 2). Then write a function called isFree that takes a total and returns true if the total is 0, otherwise false. Call both functions and print the results with console.log. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 61}, {"l": "js-3", "n": 13, "t": "Functions", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does return do in a function? Correct answer: It sends a value back to the caller and ends the function. Why: return hands a value back to the caller, and nothing after it runs. Question: What is the difference between parameters and arguments? Correct answer: Parameters are in the definition, arguments are the values passed when calling. Why: function add(a, b) has parameters a and b; add(3, 4) passes the arguments 3 and 4. Question: Which is a correct function declaration? Correct answer: function greet() { }. Why: A function declaration starts with the keyword function, then the name and parentheses.", "w": 108}, {"l": "js-4", "n": 14, "t": "DOM & Events", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "What is the DOM?", "x": "When the browser loads a page, it turns the HTML into a tree of objects called the DOM — the Document Object Model. Every tag becomes an object: the <h1> is an object, each paragraph is an object, each button is an object. JavaScript can reach into this tree, find any element, read it, change it, or delete it — and the page updates instantly, without reloading. That is the whole trick of interactive websites. HTML builds the tree, JavaScript edits it. Everything in this lesson is about finding elements and changing them.", "w": 93}, {"l": "js-4", "n": 14, "t": "DOM & Events", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Finding elements", "x": "There are two ways to find an element. getElementById finds the one element with a given id — fast and exact, because ids are unique: let title = document.getElementById(\"main-title\"); querySelector finds the first element that matches a CSS selector — an id, a class, a tag, or anything more specific: let firstButton = document.querySelector(\"button\"); let menu = document.querySelector(\".menu\"); let intro = document.querySelector(\"#intro\"); And querySelectorAll finds every matching element and returns them as a list you can loop over. If nothing matches, getElementById and querySelector return null — and trying to change null throws an error. When your script says \"cannot read properties of null\", it almost always means the id in your JavaScript does not match the id in your HTML. Check the spelling first.", "w": 125}, {"l": "js-4", "n": 14, "t": "DOM & Events", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Changing content and style", "x": "Once you have an element, you can change it. textContent changes the text inside it. style changes its CSS, one property at a time — note that CSS names with hyphens become camelCase in JavaScript (background-color becomes backgroundColor): let heading = document.getElementById(\"main-title\"); heading.textContent = \"Welcome to my page!\"; heading.style.color = \"darkblue\"; heading.style.fontSize = \"32px\"; You can also change classes instead of single styles, which is cleaner when several properties change at once. classList.add(\"hidden\") adds a class, classList.remove(\"hidden\") takes it away, and classList.toggle(\"hidden\") switches it on or off. This is how the dark-mode button on this website works — it toggles a class on the whole page. One warning: innerHTML lets you set HTML inside an element, but it is dangerous with text from users — a visitor could inject their own HTML or scripts. For your own fixed text it is fine; for anything a user typed, use textContent instead.", "w": 150}, {"l": "js-4", "n": 14, "t": "DOM & Events", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Listening for events", "x": "An event is something that happens on the page: a click, a key press, a form being submitted, the page finishing loading. addEventListener tells the browser: \"when this event happens on this element, run this function\": let button = document.getElementById(\"likeBtn\"); let count = 0; button.addEventListener(\"click\", function () { count++; button.textContent = \"Likes: \" + count; }); Notice the function has no name and no parentheses after it — you are handing the function to the browser, and the browser calls it later, every time the button is clicked. The most common events are click, input (when a field's value changes), submit (when a form is sent) and keydown. With find, listen and change, you can build almost any interaction on the web.", "w": 122}, {"l": "js-4", "n": 14, "t": "DOM & Events", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — A theme switcher", "x": "Make a page with a heading, a paragraph and a button that says 'Dark mode'. Add a script: when the button is clicked, toggle a class called dark on the body, and change the button text to 'Light mode' (and back). In your CSS, define body.dark with a dark background and light text. This is the same idea as the theme toggle on this website. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 72}, {"l": "js-4", "n": 14, "t": "DOM & Events", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does document.getElementById(\"title\") return? Correct answer: The element with that id. Why: getElementById finds the one element with the given id. Question: What does textContent change? Correct answer: The text inside an element. Why: textContent sets the text inside an element, safely, without HTML. Question: What does addEventListener(\"click\", ...) do? Correct answer: It runs a function every time the element is clicked. Why: addEventListener tells the browser to run a function when the event happens.", "w": 81}, {"l": "js-5", "n": 15, "t": "Conditions & Loops", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Making decisions with if", "x": "Real programs make decisions. \"If the password is correct, let the user in. Otherwise, show an error.\" In JavaScript, that is an if statement. The code inside the braces runs only when the condition is true: let age = 20; if (age >= 18) { console.log(\"You are an adult.\"); } else { console.log(\"You are under 18.\"); } The condition uses comparison operators: === (equal), !== (not equal), >, <, >=, <=. Notice === has three equals signs. Two equals (==) also exists, but it converts types before comparing, so \"5\" == 5 is true while \"5\" === 5 is false. The three-equals version is stricter and safer — always use ===. You can chain decisions with else if: let marks = 85; if (marks >= 90) { console.log(\"Grade: A+\"); } else if (marks >= 80) { console.log(\"Grade: A\"); } else if (marks >= 70) { console.log(\"Grade: B\"); } else { console.log(\"Grade: C or below\"); } JavaScript checks the conditions from the top and runs the first one that is true, then skips the rest. Order matters: put the most specific condition first.", "w": 182}, {"l": "js-5", "n": 15, "t": "Conditions & Loops", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Combining conditions", "x": "Real decisions usually combine two or more checks. && means \"and\" — both sides must be true. || means \"or\" — at least one side must be true. ! means \"not\" — it flips true to false: let age = 20; let hasTicket = true; if (age >= 18 && hasTicket) { console.log(\"You may enter.\"); } if (age < 18 || !hasTicket) { console.log(\"You may not enter.\"); } A common pattern is a guard: check the bad case first and stop early, so the rest of the function only runs when everything is fine. It keeps the code flat and readable instead of nesting ifs inside ifs.", "w": 107}, {"l": "js-5", "n": 15, "t": "Conditions & Loops", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Repeating with loops", "x": "A loop repeats code. The for loop is for when you know how many times to repeat. It has three parts, separated by semicolons: start, condition, and step: for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { console.log(\"Count: \" + i); } // Count: 1, Count: 2, Count: 3, Count: 4, Count: 5 Read it as: start with i at 1; while i is 5 or less, run the code, then add 1 to i. The variable i is the counter — by tradition it is called i, short for index. The while loop is for when you do not know how many times: it repeats while its condition is true, and stops when it becomes false. Be careful — if the condition never becomes false, the loop never stops, and the page freezes. Always make sure something inside the loop moves towards the exit: let number = 1; while (number < 100) { number = number * 2; } console.log(number); // 128", "w": 164}, {"l": "js-5", "n": 15, "t": "Conditions & Loops", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Looping over things", "x": "Loops are most useful for going through a list. The for...of loop takes each item in turn, with no counting needed: let fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"mango\"]; for (let fruit of fruits) { console.log(\"I like \" + fruit); } You will use this pattern constantly in the next lesson, where lists become arrays, and in the final project, where a loop draws every task in the to-do list. Conditions decide whether code runs; loops decide how many times. Together they are the two ideas that make programs useful.", "w": 88}, {"l": "js-5", "n": 15, "t": "Conditions & Loops", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — A grading program", "x": "Write a script with a variable marks (try 85, then 65, then 45). Use if / else if / else to print the grade: 90+ is A+, 80+ is A, 70+ is B, 60+ is C, otherwise F. Then write a for loop that prints the numbers 1 to 10, and a second loop that prints only the even numbers from 2 to 20. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 71}, {"l": "js-5", "n": 15, "t": "Conditions & Loops", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What is the difference between == and ===? Correct answer: == checks the value, === also checks the type. Why: \"5\" == 5 is true, but \"5\" === 5 is false, because the types differ. Question: Which loop is best when you know how many times to repeat? Correct answer: for. Why: A for loop has a start, a condition and a step, so it fits a fixed count. Question: What does && mean in a condition? Correct answer: Both sides must be true. Why: && means 'and' — both sides must be true for the whole condition to be true.", "w": 107}, {"l": "js-6", "n": 16, "t": "Arrays & Objects", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Lists with arrays", "x": "A variable holds one value. An array holds a list of values, in order, inside square brackets. Each value has a position called an index — and the first position is 0, not 1: let fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"mango\"]; console.log(fruits[0]); // \"apple\" console.log(fruits[2]); // \"mango\" console.log(fruits.length); // 3 The length property tells you how many items there are. The last item is always at index length - 1 — for three items, index 2. Asking for an index that does not exist gives undefined, not an error, so check your indexes carefully. You can change an item by its index, and you can loop over the whole list with the for...of loop from the last lesson: fruits[1] = \"orange\"; for (let fruit of fruits) { console.log(fruit); }", "w": 128}, {"l": "js-6", "n": 16, "t": "Arrays & Objects", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Array methods", "x": "Arrays come with built-in methods — functions that belong to the array. The four you will use most are push (add to the end), pop (remove from the end), indexOf (find the position of a value) and join (glue the items into one string): let tasks = [\"Learn HTML\", \"Learn CSS\"]; tasks.push(\"Learn JavaScript\"); console.log(tasks); // [\"Learn HTML\", \"Learn CSS\", \"Learn JavaScript\"] let last = tasks.pop(); console.log(last); // \"Learn JavaScript\" console.log(tasks.indexOf(\"Learn CSS\")); // 1 console.log(tasks.join(\", \")); // \"Learn HTML, Learn CSS\" There are many more methods — filter, map and forEach are the next ones to learn — but these four carry most real programs. The to-do app in Lesson 18 is built almost entirely from push, filter and join.", "w": 119}, {"l": "js-6", "n": 16, "t": "Arrays & Objects", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Objects: labelled values", "x": "An array is a list in order. An object is a set of labelled values — each value has a name, called a key. Objects describe one thing with several properties: let student = { name: \"Usman\", age: 20, city: \"Lahore\", isEnrolled: true }; console.log(student.name); // \"Usman\" console.log(student.city); // \"Lahore\" student.age = 21; // change a property You read a property with dot notation — student.name. You can add a new property the same way, by assigning to a key that does not exist yet. Objects and arrays are the two shapes almost all data takes in JavaScript: a list of things (array) where each thing has named fields (object).", "w": 110}, {"l": "js-6", "n": 16, "t": "Arrays & Objects", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Arrays of objects", "x": "Put them together and you can describe real data. A list of students, where each student is an object: let students = [ { name: \"Usman\", age: 20, city: \"Lahore\" }, { name: \"Ahmad\", age: 21, city: \"Karachi\" }, { name: \"Sara\", age: 19, city: \"Islamabad\" } ]; for (let s of students) { console.log(s.name + \" lives in \" + s.city); } This pattern — an array of objects, looped over to build a page — is behind almost every list on the internet: products in a shop, posts in a feed, messages in a chat. Learn it well, because the final project uses exactly this shape to store its tasks.", "w": 112}, {"l": "js-6", "n": 16, "t": "Arrays & Objects", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — A small shop list", "x": "Make an array of three products, each an object with name and price (for example, { name: 'Bread', price: 200 }). Loop over the array and print each product as 'Bread — Rs 200'. Then add a fourth product with push, and print the new total price by adding up all the prices in a loop. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 63}, {"l": "js-6", "n": 16, "t": "Arrays & Objects", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What is the index of the first item in an array? Correct answer: 0. Why: Arrays are zero-based: the first item is at index 0. Question: What does push() do to an array? Correct answer: It adds an item to the end. Why: push() adds a value to the end of the array. Question: How do you read the name property of an object called user? Correct answer: user.name. Why: Dot notation reads a property: user.name.", "w": 82}, {"l": "js-7", "n": 17, "t": "Fetch & APIs (intro)", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "What is an API?", "x": "So far, all your data has lived inside your own code. But real websites get their data from somewhere else: weather from a weather service, posts from a server, prices from a shop's database. An API (Application Programming Interface) is a web address you can ask for data, and it answers with data instead of a web page. For example, https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1 is a free practice API. Open it in your browser and you will see raw data — a post with a title and a body — instead of a designed page. Your JavaScript can ask for that same address, get the data, and show it on your page in your own design.", "w": 113}, {"l": "js-7", "n": 17, "t": "Fetch & APIs (intro)", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "fetch() and promises", "x": "JavaScript asks for data with fetch(). You give it a web address, and it returns a Promise — an object that says \"I do not have the answer yet, but I will\". The data takes time to travel over the internet, so JavaScript does not wait: it carries on, and the promise delivers the answer when it arrives: fetch(\"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\") .then(function (response) { console.log(response); }); .then() means \"when the answer arrives, run this function with it\". The first answer is a response object — it tells you the status (200 means OK) and carries the data. But the data is still in raw text form, so you need one more step.", "w": 110}, {"l": "js-7", "n": 17, "t": "Fetch & APIs (intro)", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Reading JSON", "x": "APIs almost always answer in JSON — JavaScript Object Notation. JSON looks like a JavaScript object, and that is no accident: it was designed to be easy for JavaScript to read. response.json() reads the body and turns it into a real object you can use: fetch(\"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\") .then(function (response) { return response.json(); }) .then(function (post) { console.log(post.title); console.log(post.body); }); Two .then() steps: the first gets the response and returns its JSON, the second receives the finished object. post.title is now a normal string you can put on the page with textContent, exactly like in Lesson 14. That is the whole pattern: fetch, read JSON, use the data. One rule: fetch only works on pages served over HTTP — it will fail if you open your HTML file directly from your computer with file://. Use a local server (for example python3 -m http.server) or a hosted page like this one, and it works.", "w": 151}, {"l": "js-7", "n": 17, "t": "Fetch & APIs (intro)", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "async and await", "x": "Chained .then() calls work, but they get hard to read when there are several steps. async and await are a cleaner way to write the same thing. await pauses the function until the promise finishes — the code reads top to bottom, like normal code: async function loadPost() { let response = await fetch( \"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\" ); let post = await response.json(); console.log(post.title); } loadPost(); The function must be marked async for await to work inside it. The page does not freeze while it waits — only this function pauses; everything else keeps running. Use async/await for new code, and recognise .then() when you see it in older code. Both do the same job.", "w": 113}, {"l": "js-7", "n": 17, "t": "Fetch & APIs (intro)", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — Show a post on your page", "x": "Make a page with an h2 and a paragraph with ids. Write an async function that fetches post 1 from https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1, reads it as JSON, and puts the post title in the h2 and the body in the paragraph. Serve the page with python3 -m http.server and open it through http://localhost:8000 — then change the URL to /posts/2 and watch the content change. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 70}, {"l": "js-7", "n": 17, "t": "Fetch & APIs (intro)", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does fetch() return? Correct answer: A Promise. Why: fetch() returns a Promise — the data arrives later, and .then() or await receives it. Question: What does response.json() do? Correct answer: It reads the response body as JSON. Why: response.json() turns the raw response body into a JavaScript object. Question: Why use async/await instead of chained .then() calls? Correct answer: The code reads top to bottom, like normal code. Why: async/await pauses the function until the promise finishes, so the code stays readable.", "w": 89}, {"l": "js-8", "n": 18, "t": "Build a To-Do App", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "The plan", "x": "This is the last JavaScript lesson, and it uses everything you have learned. We will build a to-do app: type a task, click Add, and it appears in a list. Each task has a delete button, and clicking the task marks it done. The tasks survive a page refresh, because they are saved in the browser. The plan has four parts. First, the HTML: an input, an Add button, and an empty list. Second, an array that holds the tasks. Third, a render() function that draws the list from the array. Fourth, saving the array in localStorage so it survives a refresh. When the data changes, we save it and re-draw the list — one pattern for the whole app. <input id=\"taskInput\" placeholder=\"New task...\"> <button id=\"addBtn\">Add</button> <ul id=\"taskList\"></ul>", "w": 128}, {"l": "js-8", "n": 18, "t": "Build a To-Do App", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Adding a task", "x": "When the Add button is clicked, we read the input, add the task to the array, clear the input, save, and re-draw: let tasks = []; let input = document.getElementById(\"taskInput\"); let list = document.getElementById(\"taskList\"); document.getElementById(\"addBtn\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () { let text = input.value.trim(); if (text === \"\") return; // ignore empty tasks tasks.push({ text: text, done: false }); input.value = \"\"; saveAndRender(); }); Each task is an object with two properties: the text and whether it is done. The trim() call removes spaces from both ends, so a task of only spaces is treated as empty. The guard if (text === \"\") return; stops empty tasks from being added at all.", "w": 110}, {"l": "js-8", "n": 18, "t": "Build a To-Do App", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Drawing the list", "x": "The render() function clears the list and rebuilds it from the array. For each task it creates an <li>, a text span, and a delete button, and attaches the right behaviour to each: function render() { list.innerHTML = \"\"; tasks.forEach(function (task, index) { let li = document.createElement(\"li\"); li.textContent = task.text; if (task.done) li.classList.add(\"done\"); li.addEventListener(\"click\", function () { task.done = !task.done; saveAndRender(); }); let del = document.createElement(\"button\"); del.textContent = \"Delete\"; del.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) { e.stopPropagation(); tasks.splice(index, 1); saveAndRender(); }); li.appendChild(del); list.appendChild(li); }); } Two details matter. First, document.createElement(\"li\") builds a new element in JavaScript, and appendChild puts it on the page. Second, the delete button's handler calls e.stopPropagation() — without it, clicking Delete would also trigger the list item's click handler and mark the task done just before deleting it. Events bubble up from the button to the list item, and stopPropagation stops that. splice(index, 1) removes one item at a given position from the array. And task.done = !task.done flips the done flag — ! turns true into false and false into true.", "w": 173}, {"l": "js-8", "n": 18, "t": "Build a To-Do App", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Saving with localStorage", "x": "Without saving, the tasks vanish on refresh. localStorage is a small storage area in the browser — the same one this course uses for your lesson progress. It only stores strings, so we convert the array to JSON text when saving, and parse it back when loading: function saveAndRender() { localStorage.setItem(\"todo-tasks\", JSON.stringify(tasks)); render(); } let saved = localStorage.getItem(\"todo-tasks\"); if (saved) tasks = JSON.parse(saved); render(); JSON.stringify turns the array into a string; JSON.parse turns the string back into an array. On page load, we read the saved string — if there is one — and draw the list from it. Add a task, refresh the page, and it is still there. You have just built a complete app: input, storage, rendering, and events, in about forty lines. That is what this whole module was for.", "w": 134}, {"l": "js-8", "n": 18, "t": "Build a To-Do App", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Exercise — Finish the app", "x": "Build the full to-do app from this lesson in one HTML file, with a little CSS to make done tasks show a line through them (a .done class with text-decoration: line-through). Then add one feature of your own: a 'Clear completed' button that removes all done tasks, or a counter that shows how many tasks are left. Test it, refresh the page, and confirm your tasks are still there. Try this code in the editor →", "w": 76}, {"l": "js-8", "n": 18, "t": "Build a To-Do App", "m": "JavaScript", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which array method adds a task to the end of the list? Correct answer: push(). Why: push() adds a value to the end of the array. Question: How do you save an array in localStorage? Correct answer: localStorage.setItem(\"key\", JSON.stringify(array)). Why: localStorage only stores strings, so the array must be turned into JSON text first. Question: Which DOM method creates a new element in JavaScript? Correct answer: document.createElement(). Why: document.createElement(\"li\") builds a new element, which appendChild then puts on the page.", "w": 86}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Profile Card", "x": "A profile card is the smallest real website: a photo, a name, a short line about the person, and links. You have seen them on team pages, social profiles and portfolios. In this project you build one from scratch, using the HTML and CSS from the first two modules. By the end you will have a card you can reuse on your own portfolio.", "w": 64}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 1 — The HTML structure", "x": "A card is a <div> with a photo, a heading, a paragraph and some links inside. The photo uses a placeholder image from the internet here — replace it with your own photo later: <div class=\"card\"> <img src=\"https://via.placeholder.com/150\" alt=\"Photo of Usman\"> <h2>Muhammad Usman</h2> <p class=\"role\">AI Engineer & Creator</p> <p>Building AI video ads and websites in Lahore.</p> <a href=\"https://github.com/Usmankalera-dev\">GitHub</a> <a href=\"https://usmankalera-dev.github.io/portfolio/\">Portfolio</a> </div>", "w": 61}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 2 — Card styling", "x": "Now make it look like a card: a white background on a light page, padding inside, rounded corners, and a soft shadow. The box-shadow property takes four values — how far right, how far down, how blurry, and the colour: .card { width: 320px; margin: 40px auto; padding: 24px; background: #ffffff; border-radius: 16px; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15); text-align: center; } margin: 40px auto centres the card (from the Box Model lesson), and text-align: center centres everything inside it.", "w": 82}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 3 — A round photo", "x": "Profile photos are usually round. A square image becomes a circle with two rules: make it square, then set border-radius: 50%: .card img { width: 120px; height: 120px; border-radius: 50%; object-fit: cover; border: 3px solid #f59e0b; } object-fit: cover crops the photo to fill the square instead of stretching it, so faces never look squashed. The amber border matches this website's accent colour.", "w": 63}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 4 — Style the text and links", "x": "Finish the inside of the card. The role line gets a muted colour and no top margin, and the links become small amber buttons: .role { color: #6b7280; margin-top: -8px; } .card a { display: inline-block; margin: 4px; padding: 8px 16px; background: #f59e0b; color: #111827; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 8px; font-weight: bold; } .card a:hover { background: #d97706; }", "w": 58}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 5 — Make it responsive", "x": "On a phone, a 320px card fits — but only just. Change the width to 90% with a max-width, so it fills small screens and stops growing on big ones: .card { width: 90%; max-width: 320px; } That is the whole project: structure, card styling, a round photo, buttons, and one responsive rule. Save it, open it on your phone, and you have a real profile card to show people.", "w": 70}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Build checklist", "x": "Tick each item as you finish it. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Try this project in the editor →", "w": 21}, {"l": "proj-1", "n": 19, "t": "Profile Card", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: How do you make a square image round with CSS? Correct answer: border-radius: 50%. Why: Make the image square, then border-radius: 50% turns it into a circle. Question: What does object-fit: cover do? Correct answer: It crops the photo to fill the box without stretching it. Why: object-fit: cover crops the photo to fill the square instead of stretching it, so faces never look squashed. Question: What does margin: 40px auto do to the card? Correct answer: It centres the card on the page. Why: margin: 40px auto centres a fixed-width block — the browser splits the leftover space equally on both sides.", "w": 109}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Contact Form", "x": "Every business website needs a way for visitors to get in touch. In this project you build a contact form that looks professional, checks its own inputs, and shows a success message without reloading the page — using everything from the HTML forms lesson and the JavaScript events lesson.", "w": 49}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 1 — The HTML form", "x": "Start with the form from Lesson 4, inside a card. Every field has a label, a matching id, and the required attribute: <form id=\"contactForm\"> <label for=\"cName\">Your name</label> <input type=\"text\" id=\"cName\" required> <label for=\"cEmail\">Email</label> <input type=\"email\" id=\"cEmail\" required> <label for=\"cMsg\">Message</label> <textarea id=\"cMsg\" rows=\"4\" required></textarea> <button type=\"submit\">Send message</button> <p id=\"formMsg\"></p> </form>", "w": 49}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 2 — Style the fields", "x": "Form fields look plain by default. Give them full width, comfortable padding, a soft border, and a visible focus state so keyboard users can see where they are: form { max-width: 480px; margin: 0 auto; } label { display: block; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0 4px; } input, textarea { width: 100%; padding: 12px; border: 1px solid #d1d5db; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 1rem; } input:focus, textarea:focus { outline: 2px solid #f59e0b; border-color: #f59e0b; } display: block on the labels puts each label on its own line above its field. width: 100% on the inputs makes them fill the form — remember box-sizing: border-box from the Box Model lesson, so the padding does not make them overflow.", "w": 115}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 3 — Style the button", "x": "The submit button gets the amber style used across this site, full width so it is easy to tap on a phone: button[type=\"submit\"] { width: 100%; margin-top: 16px; padding: 12px; background: #f59e0b; border: none; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 1rem; font-weight: bold; cursor: pointer; } button[type=\"submit\"]:hover { background: #d97706; }", "w": 48}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 4 — Validate with JavaScript", "x": "The required attributes already stop empty submissions. Now add a JavaScript check of your own: on submit, stop the page from reloading with preventDefault(), and check that the message is at least 10 characters long: let form = document.getElementById(\"contactForm\"); let msg = document.getElementById(\"formMsg\"); form.addEventListener(\"submit\", function (e) { e.preventDefault(); let text = document.getElementById(\"cMsg\").value.trim(); if (text.length < 10) { msg.textContent = \"Please write a message of at least 10 characters.\"; msg.style.color = \"red\"; return; } msg.textContent = \"Thank you! Your message has been sent.\"; msg.style.color = \"green\"; form.reset(); }); e.preventDefault() is the key line: without it, the browser would try to send the form and reload the page. With it, JavaScript stays in control. form.reset() clears all the fields after a successful send.", "w": 121}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 5 — Test it", "x": "Open the page and try three things: submit it empty (the browser should point at the first empty field), submit it with a one-word message (your JavaScript error should appear), then submit it properly (the green success message should appear and the fields should clear). If all three work, your form behaves like a real one — the only missing piece is a server to receive the data, which is a later course.", "w": 73}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Build checklist", "x": "Tick each item as you finish it. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Try this project in the editor →", "w": 21}, {"l": "proj-2", "n": 20, "t": "Contact Form", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does e.preventDefault() do in the submit handler? Correct answer: It stops the page from reloading so JavaScript stays in control. Why: Without preventDefault(), the browser would try to send the form and reload the page. Question: Which check makes sure the message is at least 10 characters long? Correct answer: text.length < 10. Why: The code checks text.length < 10 and shows an error if the message is too short. Question: What does form.reset() do after a successful send? Correct answer: It clears all the fields. Why: form.reset() clears all the fields after a successful send.", "w": 103}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Navbar", "x": "The navigation bar is the part of a website visitors use most, and it is also the part beginners get wrong most often. In this project you build a proper navbar: logo on the left, links on the right, a sticky position so it stays visible while scrolling, and a layout that still works on a phone — using Flexbox from Lesson 9.", "w": 63}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 1 — The HTML", "x": "A navbar is a <header> with a logo link and a <nav> holding the page links: <header class=\"navbar\"> <a class=\"logo\" href=\"#\">My Bakery</a> <nav> <a href=\"#\">Home</a> <a href=\"#\">Menu</a> <a href=\"#\">About</a> <a href=\"#\">Contact</a> </nav> </header>", "w": 33}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 2 — Flexbox layout", "x": "The header is a flex container. justify-content: space-between pushes the logo to the left and the nav to the right, and align-items: center lines them up vertically. The nav is itself a flex container, so its links sit in a row with a gap: .navbar { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: 12px 20px; background: #111827; } .navbar nav { display: flex; gap: 20px; } .navbar a { color: #f9fafb; text-decoration: none; }", "w": 74}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 3 — Style the logo and links", "x": "Make the logo stand out — bigger, bolder, amber — and give the links a hover effect so visitors can see what they are about to click: .navbar .logo { font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 800; color: #f59e0b; } .navbar nav a { padding: 8px 10px; border-radius: 8px; } .navbar nav a:hover { background: #374151; }", "w": 54}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 4 — Make it sticky", "x": "A sticky navbar stays at the top of the screen while the visitor scrolls, so the navigation is always one tap away. One line does it: .navbar { position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50; } position: sticky means \"scroll normally, but stop at the top of the screen and stay there\". top: 0 says where it stops. z-index: 50 makes sure the bar floats above the page content instead of sliding underneath it. This website's own header uses exactly these three lines.", "w": 82}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 5 — Mobile layout", "x": "On a phone, four links plus a logo may not fit in one row. Add a media query that lets the bar wrap, and shrinks the gaps: @media (max-width: 600px) { .navbar { flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; } .navbar nav { gap: 8px; } .navbar nav a { padding: 6px 8px; } } With flex-wrap: wrap, the links drop onto a second line instead of squeezing or overflowing. Test it by shrinking your browser window: the bar should stay readable at every size, with no horizontal scrolling.", "w": 87}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Build checklist", "x": "Tick each item as you finish it. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Try this project in the editor →", "w": 21}, {"l": "proj-3", "n": 21, "t": "Navbar", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: Which property pushes the logo to the left and the nav to the right? Correct answer: justify-content: space-between. Why: justify-content: space-between pushes the logo to the left and the nav to the right. Question: What does position: sticky with top: 0 do? Correct answer: The bar scrolls normally, then stops at the top and stays there. Why: position: sticky means 'scroll normally, but stop at the top of the screen and stay there'. Question: Why does the navbar use z-index: 50? Correct answer: So the bar floats above the page content instead of sliding underneath it. Why: z-index: 50 makes sure the bar floats above the page content instead of sliding underneath it.", "w": 119}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Image Gallery", "x": "A photo gallery is a grid of images with captions — a photographer's portfolio, a shop's products, a travel blog's best shots. In this project you build one with CSS Grid, a layout system made for two-dimensional grids. The pictures here are coloured placeholder blocks: replace each one with your own photos, and the gallery is ready to show people.", "w": 60}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 1 — The HTML: figures in a grid", "x": "Each picture is a <figure> with a coloured block standing in for a photo, and a <figcaption> underneath. Six of them go inside a gallery container: <div class=\"gallery\"> <figure> <div class=\"photo\" style=\"background: #f59e0b;\">Hunza</div> <figcaption>Hunza valley in autumn</figcaption> </figure> <figure> <div class=\"photo\" style=\"background: #3b82f6;\">Sea</div> <figcaption>The sea at Clifton beach</figcaption> </figure> <!-- four more figures the same way --> </div> The style attribute here only sets the placeholder colour. When you use real photos, replace each <div class=\"photo\"> with an <img> tag — the CSS below works for both.", "w": 87}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 2 — CSS Grid layout", "x": "CSS Grid is like Flexbox, but for two dimensions at once — rows and columns together. display: grid turns the container into a grid, and grid-template-columns defines the columns. The magic value is repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr)): \"make as many columns as fit, each at least 200px wide, sharing the space equally\": .gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; } On a laptop that gives three or four columns. On a phone it gives one or two — automatically, with no media query. 1fr means \"one fraction of the free space\", so the columns always share the width evenly.", "w": 101}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 3 — Style the pictures and captions", "x": "Give each picture a fixed height and rounded corners, and centre the caption text underneath: .photo { height: 160px; border-radius: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; color: white; font-weight: bold; font-size: 1.2rem; } .gallery figcaption { text-align: center; margin-top: 8px; color: #4b5563; } For real photos, add width: 100%; height: 160px; object-fit: cover; to the img instead — the picture fills the box without stretching, exactly like the round profile photo in Project 1.", "w": 75}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 4 — A hover effect", "x": "A small hover effect makes the gallery feel alive. When the mouse is over a figure, the picture lifts slightly and grows a soft shadow. The transition property makes the change smooth instead of instant: .gallery figure { margin: 0; transition: transform 0.2s ease; } .gallery figure:hover { transform: translateY(-4px); } .gallery figure:hover .photo { box-shadow: 0 8px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2); } transform: translateY(-4px) moves the whole figure 4 pixels up. Transforms do not disturb the layout around them — the other pictures stay exactly where they are.", "w": 90}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 5 — Responsive check", "x": "Shrink your browser window and watch the grid: columns should drop from four to three to two to one, with no horizontal scrolling and no squashed pictures. If a column looks too narrow on a phone, lower the minimum in minmax() from 200px to 150px. That one number controls the whole responsive behaviour — change it and the grid reflows itself.", "w": 61}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Build checklist", "x": "Tick each item as you finish it. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Try this project in the editor →", "w": 21}, {"l": "proj-4", "n": 22, "t": "Image Gallery", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr)) do? Correct answer: It makes as many columns as fit, each at least 200px wide. Why: It means 'make as many columns as fit, each at least 200px wide, sharing the space equally'. Question: Which tag holds a picture's caption? Correct answer: <figcaption>. Why: Each picture is a <figure> with a <figcaption> underneath it. Question: What does transform: translateY(-4px) do on hover? Correct answer: It moves the figure 4 pixels up. Why: transform: translateY(-4px) moves the whole figure 4 pixels up — and transforms do not disturb the layout around them.", "w": 102}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "To-Do App", "x": "This is the final project of the course, and it brings the whole JavaScript module together: the DOM, events, arrays, objects and localStorage, all in one working app. You built the pieces in Lesson 18 — now you assemble them into a polished page with styling, a counter, and a clear-completed button. When this project works, you can honestly say you built a real web app.", "w": 66}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 1 — The HTML shell", "x": "The app needs an input, an Add button, a counter, a list, and a Clear completed button: <div class=\"todo-app\"> <h2>My Tasks</h2> <input id=\"taskInput\" placeholder=\"New task...\"> <button id=\"addBtn\">Add</button> <p id=\"counter\">0 tasks left</p> <ul id=\"taskList\"></ul> <button id=\"clearBtn\">Clear completed</button> </div>", "w": 37}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 2 — Style the app", "x": "Give the app a card look, and make done tasks show a line through them — the visual signal that a task is finished: .todo-app { max-width: 480px; margin: 0 auto; background: #ffffff; padding: 24px; border-radius: 16px; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); } #taskList { list-style: none; padding: 0; } #taskList li { padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb; cursor: pointer; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; } #taskList li.done { text-decoration: line-through; color: #9ca3af; }", "w": 79}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 3 — Add and render tasks", "x": "The JavaScript is the same pattern as Lesson 18: an array of task objects, a render() function that rebuilds the list, and an Add handler that pushes a new task: let tasks = []; let input = document.getElementById(\"taskInput\"); let list = document.getElementById(\"taskList\"); let counter = document.getElementById(\"counter\"); function render() { list.innerHTML = \"\"; let left = 0; tasks.forEach(function (task, index) { if (!task.done) left++; let li = document.createElement(\"li\"); li.textContent = task.text; if (task.done) li.classList.add(\"done\"); li.addEventListener(\"click\", function () { task.done = !task.done; saveAndRender(); }); let del = document.createElement(\"button\"); del.textContent = \"Delete\"; del.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) { e.stopPropagation(); tasks.splice(index, 1); saveAndRender(); }); li.appendChild(del); list.appendChild(li); }); counter.textContent = left + \" task\" + (left === 1 ? \"\" : \"s\") + \" left\"; } document.getElementById(\"addBtn\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () { let text = input.value.trim(); if (text === \"\") return; tasks.push({ text: text, done: false }); input.value = \"\"; saveAndRender(); }); The counter counts only tasks that are not done, and the word \"task\" becomes \"tasks\" when the count is not 1 — a small detail that makes the app feel finished.", "w": 174}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 4 — Clear completed and save", "x": "The Clear completed button keeps only the tasks that are not done, using filter — a method that returns a new array with only the items that pass a test: document.getElementById(\"clearBtn\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () { tasks = tasks.filter(function (task) { return !task.done; }); saveAndRender(); }); function saveAndRender() { localStorage.setItem(\"todo-tasks\", JSON.stringify(tasks)); render(); } let saved = localStorage.getItem(\"todo-tasks\"); if (saved) tasks = JSON.parse(saved); render(); filter is worth learning properly: it takes a test function, runs it on every item, and keeps only the ones where the test returns true. Here the test is !task.done — keep the task if it is not done.", "w": 100}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Step 5 — Test the whole app", "x": "Open the page and run through the full test: add three tasks, click one to mark it done, delete another, click Clear completed, and check the counter. Then refresh the page — your remaining tasks must still be there, because of localStorage. If everything works, you have built a complete web application. Add it to your portfolio: it is the strongest proof of what this course taught you.", "w": 68}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Build checklist", "x": "Tick each item as you finish it. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Try this project in the editor →", "w": 21}, {"l": "proj-5", "n": 23, "t": "To-Do App", "m": "Mini Projects", "h": "Quiz", "x": "Quiz questions for this lesson. Question: What does filter do in the Clear completed button? Correct answer: It returns a new array with only the items that pass a test. Why: filter takes a test function, runs it on every item, and keeps only the ones where the test returns true. Question: Why is the task list saved with JSON.stringify? Correct answer: localStorage only stores strings. Why: localStorage only stores strings, so the array must be turned into JSON text first. Question: Which CSS rule puts a line through a finished task? Correct answer: #taskList li.done { text-decoration: line-through; }. Why: #taskList li.done gives finished tasks a line-through style — the visual signal that a task is done.", "w": 118}]};
